WEBVTT

NOTE What is friction? (video-friction-what-is). Generated from shared/media/videoSpecs.ts; do not edit by hand.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.200
Have you ever pushed a box and watched it stop all by itself?

2
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.800
Let's find out why!

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.200
Give a box a push, then let go.

4
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:15.200
It slides… slows down… and stops.

5
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.800
Nobody touched it after the push. Something is pushing back!

6
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:21.800
Look closely. Really closely!

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.600
Even smooth floors have tiny bumps.

8
00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.800
When the box slides, its bumps catch on the floor's bumps.

9
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.900
All that catching makes a force called friction.

10
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.400
Friction pushes back on the moving box.

11
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.800
Friction always pushes against the way things move.

12
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.000
Same push, different floors.

13
00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.400
Rough carpet makes lots of friction. The box stops quickly.

14
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.200
Wood makes some friction.

15
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.100
Smooth ice makes very little friction.

16
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.800
Less friction, so the box slides farthest on ice!

17
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.200
Let's recap. Friction is a force between surfaces that touch.

18
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.800
It pushes against motion and slows things down.

19
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:57.400
Rough surfaces make more friction. Smooth surfaces make less.

20
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Your turn! Where would a box slide farther: on ice or on carpet?

21
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:02.000
Pause and think, then try the quiz!
